export const TOPICS: Topic[] = [{"id": "bridges", "icon": "language-outline", "title": "Arabic is already in your kitchen", "hook": "Words you already say came from Arabic. Tap one to hear it.", "parts": [{"t": "p", "text": "When goods travelled around the Mediterranean, words travelled with them. Italian picked up Arabic words through trade, and Sicilian through centuries of living side by side. Each card shows the Arabic word, what it became, and the story."}, {"t": "bridges"}, {"t": "note", "text": "Honest scale check: about 300 Sicilian words come from Arabic, mostly farming and water words. Spanish borrowed far more. Arabic didn't replace Sicilian; it seasoned it."}, {"t": "p", "text": "Spot a pattern: Arabic 'the' is al-, and it often got glued to the front. Italian albicocca (apricot) and algebra both keep it. When you meet al- in this app, think 'the'."}]}, {"id": "sicily", "icon": "business-outline", "title": "When Palermo spoke Arabic", "hook": "Sicily was part of the Arabic-speaking world for over two centuries.", "parts": [{"t": "timeline", "items": [{"when": "827", "text": "Muslim forces from Ifriqiya (roughly today's Tunisia) land in Sicily and win at Mazara del Vallo on 15 July."}, {"when": "831", "text": "Palermo falls after a year-long siege and becomes the capital, renamed al-Madiina."}, {"when": "902", "text": "The fall of Taormina completes the conquest of the island (a few holdouts, such as Rometta, last until 965)."}, {"when": "1072", "text": "Palermo falls to the Normans after a five-month siege."}, {"when": "1091", "text": "Noto, Butera and Malta fall, ending independent Islamic rule in Sicily."}]}, {"t": "p", "text": "Daily life was multilingual: Classical Arabic was the official language, alongside Sicilian, Byzantine Greek, Berber and Judeo-Arabic. Arabic stayed a language of government for about a century into Norman rule, and Muslims were probably still a majority of the island's people until at least the late 12th century."}, {"t": "p", "text": "In the fields: the Arab period is credited with introducing or spreading oranges, lemons, pistachio, sugarcane, dates and mulberries, and with better irrigation by qanats. The Sicilian words zàgara (blossom), saia (canal) and zuccu (market) are Arabic."}, {"t": "bullets", "items": ["Caltanissetta: Arabic Qal'at an-nisaa', 'castle of the women'.", "Bibbirria, a gate of Agrigento: Arabic bab ar-riyaah, 'Gate of the Winds'."]}, {"t": "note", "text": "Think layers, not replacement: Arab, Byzantine Greek, Norman and Latin worlds shared this one island."}]}, {"id": "maltese", "icon": "boat-outline", "title": "Maltese: Arabic in Italian clothes", "hook": "A living language that sounds like your two interests at once.", "parts": [{"t": "p", "text": "Maltese descends from Siculo-Arabic, a Maghrebi Arabic dialect that developed in Muslim Sicily (831–1091). It is now the only standardised Semitic language written entirely in Latin letters, and the only Semitic language official in the European Union."}, {"t": "facts", "rows": [{"label": "Speakers", "value": "roughly half a million (sources give 530,000–570,000)"}, {"label": "Vocabulary", "value": "about 52% Romance (Sicilian and Italian), about 32% Arabic"}, {"label": "But", "value": "most of the core, everyday words and grammar words are Arabic"}, {"label": "Official", "value": "spelling standard 1924, official language 1934"}]}, {"t": "p", "text": "Same word, two spellings. Tap the Arabic to hear it:"}, {"t": "pairs", "items": [{"left": "raġel (man)", "word": {"latin": "rajul", "script": "رَجُل", "en": "man"}}, {"left": "tifel (boy)", "word": {"latin": "tifl", "script": "طِفْل", "en": "child / boy"}}, {"left": "dar (house)", "word": {"latin": "daar", "script": "دَار", "en": "house"}}, {"left": "xemx (sun; x = sh)", "word": {"latin": "shams", "script": "شَمْس", "en": "sun"}}, {"left": "sajf (summer)", "word": {"latin": "sayf", "script": "صَيْف", "en": "summer"}}]}, {"t": "note", "text": "Maltese is the living descendant of Sicily's Arabic: it kept an Arabic core while borrowing Sicilian and Italian vocabulary."}]}, {"id": "sea", "icon": "compass-outline", "title": "The sea that connected everything", "hook": "From Carthage to Fibonacci: the Mediterranean as one long conversation.", "parts": [{"t": "timeline", "items": [{"when": "before c. 1050 BC", "text": "Phoenician, Hebrew and other Canaanite dialects are still hard to tell apart."}, {"when": "814 BC (traditional)", "text": "Phoenicians from Tyre found Carthage. They also settle at Motya and Lilybaeum (modern Marsala) in Sicily, and in Malta."}, {"when": "c. 800s–900s", "text": "In Abbasid Baghdad scholars translate Greek works (Aristotle, Galen, Euclid, Ptolemy) into Arabic over about two centuries."}, {"when": "827–1091", "text": "Sicily is part of the Arabic-speaking world (see the Sicily card)."}, {"when": "1138–1154", "text": "In Palermo, al-Idrisi makes a 70-map atlas of the known world for Roger II, finished in 1154."}, {"when": "1202", "text": "Fibonacci's Liber Abaci introduces the Hindu–Arabic numerals to Europe."}, {"when": "1973", "text": "Arabic becomes the sixth official language of the United Nations."}]}, {"t": "p", "text": "Distant cousins: Phoenician, the language of Carthage, and Arabic are both Semitic languages. The Phoenician alphabet is the ancestor of the Greek alphabet, and through Greek of Latin; it also influenced Syriac and Arabic writing."}, {"t": "p", "text": "Ideas travelled too. Historians debate how formal Baghdad's famous 'House of Wisdom' really was, but the translation of Greek science and philosophy into Arabic is well documented. And a boy from Pisa, Leonardo Fibonacci, was educated in Bugia (now Béjaïa, Algeria), where his father ran a trading post, and learned the numerals Europe later adopted."}, {"t": "p", "text": "Even small words made the trip: Greek pandokheion became Arabic funduq, then Italian fondaco."}]}, {"id": "homes", "icon": "earth-outline", "title": "One language, many homes", "hook": "Same Arabic, very different street life.", "parts": [{"t": "p", "text": "Modern Standard Arabic (MSA) is what you read in books and newspapers and hear in news and formal speech, and what schools teach. Everyday speech is in dialects, learned at home and mostly unwritten. The words and verb patterns in this app are MSA-style, which people understand everywhere; each home dialect then bends them."}, {"t": "greetings"}, {"t": "regions"}, {"t": "note", "text": "Broad strokes, not rules: every country and city differs. Some linguists call the dialects separate languages, while Arabic speakers generally say they speak one language."}]}, {"id": "use", "icon": "airplane-outline", "title": "Where you'd actually use it", "hook": "Real situations, starting with your own neighbourhood.", "parts": [{"t": "facts", "rows": [{"label": "Native speakers", "value": "about 411 million"}, {"label": "Second-language users", "value": "about 70 million more"}, {"label": "Official in", "value": "24 states and territories"}, {"label": "United Nations", "value": "official language since 1973 (World Arabic Language Day, 18 December)"}]}, {"t": "scenarios"}, {"t": "p", "text": "Italy, end of 2024 (Italian Ministry of Labour): 377,554 Moroccan, 175,236 Egyptian and 112,486 Tunisian citizens were legally resident. Most speak a local dialect at home, so what you hear will sound different from MSA. See 'One language, many homes'."}, {"t": "p", "text": "Try this week. Small, real, doable. Tap to tick them off:"}, {"t": "missions"}]}, {"id": "roots", "icon": "git-branch-outline", "title": "The secret that makes Arabic learnable", "hook": "Learn one root, unlock a whole family of words.", "parts": [{"t": "p", "text": "Many Arabic words are built from a root of consonants (often three) fitted into vowel patterns. Learn the root once and you recognise the whole family. Tap to hear each word."}, {"t": "roots"}, {"t": "p", "text": "This is why Conjugate practice pays off twice: the same patterns that change a verb for 'I' or 'you' also build nouns, so new words stop feeling random."}, {"t": "note", "text": "When it feels hard: new sounds and patterns with few familiar cousins make Arabic feel slow at first, and that is normal. It is also why early wins feel big. Ten minutes a day beats a long Sunday. You only need the next small step."}]}];

